🔐 One of the biggest misconceptions about Post-Quantum Cryptography is that the migration challenge is primarily about algorithms.

It isn't.

The industry spends a lot of time discussing ML-KEM, ML-DSA, hybrid TLS, cryptographic standards, and quantum-resistant implementations—and rightly so.

But in large enterprise environments, those technologies are often only a small part of the problem.

The real challenge emerges when organizations attempt to answer a much more fundamental question:

**"Do we actually understand our trust estate?"**

Modern enterprises operate on thousands of interconnected trust relationships:

• Certificates and certificate chains
• TLS and mTLS infrastructure
• Identity providers and federation services
• Third-party vendor integrations
• PKI hierarchies and trust anchors
• Legacy systems and middleware
• Applications, APIs, workloads, and data flows

Most organizations maintain some form of cryptographic inventory.

Far fewer maintain a complete trust inventory.

And that difference matters.

When migration programs encounter unexpected delays, outages, or interoperability issues, the root cause is often not the quantum-safe algorithm itself.

It is an undocumented dependency.

A forgotten trust relationship.

An unknown certificate path.

An unmanaged trust anchor.

Or a critical service that nobody realized depended on a particular trust chain.

That is why successful PQC migration requires more than cryptographic modernization.

It requires trust-state discovery.

A framework I keep returning to is:

👁️ Visibility → 👤 Ownership → 🔗 Dependencies → ⚠️ Risk → 🚦 Migration Gate

Before you migrate anything:

✔️ Discover what exists

✔️ Determine who owns it

✔️ Map what depends on it

✔️ Understand the operational risk

✔️ Validate rollback and recovery paths

Only then should migration proceed.

The future of quantum readiness is not just deploying stronger cryptography.

It's gaining enough visibility and assurance to understand the trust ecosystem that already powers the enterprise.

Because in many cases, the biggest risk isn't PQC.

The biggest risk is the trust architecture you've been running for years without realizing how interconnected it really is.

